1. Current Size
Singapore is ageing faster than almost any country in Southeast Asia. As of 2025, the elderly population has crossed a symbolic threshold.
MetricFigureResidents aged 65+~789,580 (2025)Share of resident population18.8% (2025) vs. 13.1% in 2015MilestoneFirst ASEAN nation to exceed 20% elderly citizenryTotal population6.11 million (2025)
Source: Singapore Department of Statistics, Population Trends 2025

Key finding: In just 10 years (2015–2025), the share of citizens aged 65+ rose from 13.1% to 20.7% — a near-doubling driven by the baby boomer cohort entering old age.

1.2 Projections: Next 5–10 Years
The pace of ageing will accelerate further. By 2030, Singapore becomes a 'super-aged' nation (defined as >21% aged 65+), and by 2035, roughly one in three citizens will be elderly.

Key structural drivers: Singapore's Total Fertility Rate sits at ~0.97 (one of the lowest globally), meaning fewer young people enter the population each year while the elderly cohort grows. The old-age support ratio (working-age individuals per elderly person) is projected to drop from 5 today to fewer than 2 by 2050.

2. Global Elderly Population (65+)

2.1 Current Scale
Population ageing is a worldwide megatrend. As of 2024, there are approximately 830 million people aged 65 and above globally, representing about 10.2% of the world's 8.2 billion population.
Source: UN World Population Prospects 2024; Our World in Data; UNFPA

Key finding: Between 1974 and 2024, the global share of people aged 65 nearly doubled (5.5% → 10.3%). It is projected to double again by 2074, reaching 20.7%. (UNFPA, 2024)

2.2 Regional Patterns
While ageing is a universal phenomenon, Asia is now at the forefront — particularly East and Southeast Asia.

Europe and Northern America currently have the highest proportions of elderly (typically 18–23%)
Asia is catching up fastest: Hong Kong, South Korea and Japan are projected to have the highest shares of elderly (close to 40%) by 2050
Singapore leads Southeast Asia, already past 20% — no other ASEAN nation is close
Sub-Saharan Africa remains young, but will see rapid absolute growth in elderly numbers

